'''Tukituki''' is a New Zealand parliamentary [[New Zealand electorates|electorate]] in [[Hawke's Bay]]. It returns one [[Member of parliament|member of Parliament]] to the [[New Zealand House of Representatives]]. It was established for the [[1996 New Zealand general election|1996 general election]] and is named after the [[Tukituki River]]. Neighbouring electorates are [[Napier (New Zealand electorate)|Napier]] to the north, [[Wairarapa (electorate)|Wairarapa]] to the south, and [[Rangitīkei (electorate)|Rangitīkei]] over the ranges to the west.

The current member of Parliament is [[Catherine Wedd]] of the [[New Zealand National Party|National Party]], who won the seat from first-term [[New Zealand Labour Party|Labour]] MP [[Anna Lorck]] at the [[2023 New Zealand general election|2023 general election]].

==Population centres==
{{maplink|from=New Zealand Electorates/2020/General/Tukituki.map|frame=yes|text=Map of boundaries at the [[2020 New Zealand general election|2020]] and [[2023 New Zealand general election|2023]] general elections}}
Tukituki was created ahead of the change to [[Mixed-member proportional representation|mixed-member proportional]] (MMP) voting at the [[1996 New Zealand general election|1996 election]]; it was a merger of the old [[Hastings (New Zealand electorate)|Hastings]] seat with [[Central Hawke's Bay District]]. Tukituki centres on the southern [[Hawke's Bay Region|Hawke's Bay]] region, with the bulk of the electorate's population coming from the city of [[Hastings, New Zealand|Hastings]], with the rural towns of [[Havelock North]], [[Flaxmere]], [[Clive, New Zealand|Clive]] and [[Ōtāne]], and the coastal communities of [[Haumoana]], [[Te Awanga]], [[Ocean Beach, Hawke's Bay|Ocean Beach]] and [[Waimārama]] drafted in to bring the electorate up to the required population. In 2008, a general northwards tug on boundaries in the [[Taranaki]], [[Manawatū-Whanganui]] and Hawke's Bay regions saw [[Waipukurau]] and [[Waipawa]] moved into the [[Wairarapa (New Zealand electorate)|Wairarapa]] electorate, in exchange for which Tukituki gained the suburbs and towns around [[Cape Kidnappers]] from the [[Napier (New Zealand electorate)|Napier electorate]].<ref>{{cite book |title=Report of the Representation Commission 2007 |url= http://www.elections.org.nz/sites/default/files/2007%20Representation%20Commission%20Report.pdf |isbn=978-0-477-10414-2 |publisher=Representation Commission |access-date=4 October 2014 |page=9 |format=PDF |date=14 September 2007 |url-status=dead |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20141006093020/http://www.elections.org.nz/sites/default/files/2007%20Representation%20Commission%20Report.pdf |archive-date=6 October 2014}}</ref> No boundary adjustments were undertaken in the subsequent 2013/14 and 2020 redistributions.<ref name="parllib">{{cite web| url= https://www3.parliament.nz/en/mps-and-electorates/electorate-profiles/tukituki-electorate-profile/ |title=Tukituki – Electorate Profile |publisher= Parliamentary Library |date= 27 April 2021 |access-date= 14 October 2025}}</ref><ref>{{cite book| title=Report of the Representation Commission 2014 |url= http://www.elections.org.nz/sites/default/files/bulk-upload/documents/report_of_the_representation_commission_2014.pdf |isbn=978-0-477-10414-2 |publisher=Representation Commission |access-date=4 October 2014 |page=9 |format=PDF |date=4 April 2014}}</ref> In the 2025 boundary review, the electorate lost the rural communities north of the [[Ngaruroro River]] to Napier.<ref>{{cite book |title=Report of the Representation Commission 2025 |url=https://elections.nz/assets/pagecomponent-file-files/REPORT-OF-THE-REPRESENTATION-COMMISSION-2025-WEB.pdf |isbn=978-0-473-75233-0 |publisher=Representation Commission |access-date=17 September 2025 |page=8 |date=8 August 2025 }}</ref>

==History==
Labour's [[Rick Barker]],<ref>[http://www.parliament.nz/en-NZ/MPP/MPs/MPs/a/7/9/48MP171-Barker-Rick.htm New Zealand Parliament – Rick Barker MP]</ref> who had represented Hastings since [[1993 New Zealand general election|1993]], was elected as MP, and re-elected twice. National's [[Craig Foss]]<ref name="Hon Craig Foss">{{cite web |title=Hon Craig Foss |url= http://www.parliament.nz/en-nz/mpp/mps/current/51MP2401/foss-hon-craig |publisher=[[New Zealand Parliament]] |access-date=1 December 2013}}</ref> first contested the electorate in the {{NZ election link|2002}}, but Barker comfortably held it.<ref>{{cite web| title=Official Count Results – Tukituki | url= http://www.electionresults.govt.nz/electionresults_2002/electorate-54.html |access-date=1 December 2013|publisher=[[Electoral Commission (New Zealand)|Electoral Commission]]}}</ref> Ranked 47th on National's party list, Foss did not enter Parliament.<ref>{{cite web |title=Party Lists of Successful Registered Parties |url= http://www.electionresults.govt.nz/electionresults_2002/e9/html/e9_part3.html |publisher=[[Electoral Commission (New Zealand)|Electoral Commission]] |access-date=26 August 2013}}</ref>

With a large provincial swing to the [[New Zealand National Party|National Party]] in the {{NZ election link|2005}}, Foss took the seat.<ref name="2005 election" /> This was the third time in over thirty years that a Hastings electorate had elected a National MP – the other two times being National's landslide victories in [[1975 New Zealand general election|1975]] and [[1990 New Zealand general election|1990]]. Foss was returned to Parliament with a greatly increased majority in the [[2008 New Zealand general election|2008 election]].<ref name="2008 election" /> His majority increased to nearly 10,000 votes in the {{NZ election link|2011}}.<ref name="2011 election" /> In the {{NZ election link|2014}}, his majority dropped to 6,490 votes.<ref>{{cite web| url= http://electionresults.org.nz/electionresults_2014/electorate-54.html |title=Official Count Results – Tukituki |date=10 October 2014 |access-date=10 October 2014 |publisher=[[Electoral Commission (New Zealand)|Electoral Commission]]}}</ref> Foss quit politics at the [[2017 New Zealand general election|2017 general election]]<ref>{{cite news |url= http://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/news/article.cfm?c_id=1&objectid=11766805 |access-date=27 September 2017 |title=National MP Craig Foss quits politics|date=14 December 2016|work=[[The New Zealand Herald]]}}</ref> and [[Lawrence Yule]] was elected, retaining it for the National Party.

[[Anna Lorck]] narrowly took the seat off of Yule in the [[2020 New Zealand general election|2020 election]], returning it to Labour after 15 years with National.<ref>{{Cite web |date=17 October 2020 |title=Election 2020: Labour takes candidate and party vote victory in Tukituki |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/hawkes-bay-today/news/election-2020-labour-takes-candidate-and-party-vote-victory-in-tukituki/4S3BEJL7UUEWHJ2ISLDKBV4NEI/ |access-date=16 August 2024 |website=Hawke's Bay Today (via NZHerald)}}</ref>

The [[2023 New Zealand general election|2023 election]] saw [[Catherine Wedd]] re-capture the electorate back from Labour for National. ''[[Hawke's Bay Today]]'' described it as part of a "blue wave" across Hawke's Bay; Wedd overturned a 1,590 Labour majority, with a margin of 10,118 votes.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Hageman |first=Mitchell |date=15 October 2023 |title=Tukituki election 2023 results: First-time MP Catherine Wedd follows in grandfather's footsteps, vows to strengthen horticulture sector in Hawke's Bay |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/hawkes-bay-today/news/tukituki-election-2023-results-first-time-mp-catherine-wedd-follows-in-grandfathers-footsteps-vows-to-strengthen-horticulture-sector-in-hawkes-bay/4ENFFFRK7ZGP3BAT5FHQLZKQEI/ |access-date=16 August 2024 |website=Hawke's Bay Today (via NZHerald)}}</ref>

